WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.840
INTRO:
It’s no secret that pilots at the major

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.750
airlines earn fat salaries.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.260
At Delta, even new first officers earn $92
per hour.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.429
This number goes all the way up to $354 per
hour for the most experienced captains.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:20.820
A very similar payscale can be found at American
and United as well who also pay anywhere between

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.180
$90 per hour and roughly $350 per hour.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.460
Now, it should be noted that pilots don’t
work 2000 payable hours per year like most

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.460
other jobs.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.609
The average pilot flies 75 hours per month
or about 900 hours per year.

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:41.540
This translates to about $81,000 for new first
officers and $318,000 for the top captains

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.510
and that’s just cash compensation.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.899
All of these companies have extremely generous
retirement plans that are idiot proof.

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:53.440
Usually, most companies will match your retirement
contributions up to a certain amount, but

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.940
if you don’t contribute, they won’t contribute
either.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.559
All three of these airlines, however, have
a defined direct contribution of 16% into

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:04.440
either a 401K plan or a B fund.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.140
This means that on top of your salary, they’ll
contribute 16% worth of your salary into one

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.650
of these retirement funds.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:16.170
Additionally, they also offer 401K matching
as well usually up to 5-6%.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.800
This means that even as new first officer,
you’ll automatically be contributing about

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.680
$13,000 per year into a 401K plan thanks to
the defined contribution.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.620
If you maximize your own contribution to the
limit of $20,500, with the 401K match, you’d

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.440
be contributing about $35,000 on just your
first year.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.070
For perspective, if you contribute $35,000
into your 401k for 40 years at an 8% return,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.960
you’re left with $9 million.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.770
And that’s literally the lower bound.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:52.860
For the most experienced captains, they enjoy
a defined contribution itself of roughly $50,000.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.990
At this point, they only need to contribute
$11,000 from their own paycheck to meet the

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.760
total contribution limit of $61,000.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.630
At this rate, you’d be looking at reaching
$15 million by retirement.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.750
On top of all this, pilots at major airlines
also enjoy generous profit sharing.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:15.260
Before the pandemic started, Delta shared
a record $1.6 billion which works out to about

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.090
$17,500 per employee.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.360
When you add all of this up, you’ll see
that these pilots start off in the low $100s

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.380
and are able to work up all the way to nearly
$400,000 in total compensation.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.130
And the best part is that, progression within
the airline industry is extremely straight

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.130
forward.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.280
You don’t have to suck up to your boss or
come up with a new product idea to get a promotion.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.670
Promotions within the aviation industry are
generally automated and solely dependent on

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.830
your total flight hours and experience.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.190
So, if you stick with it for long enough,
you will eventually reach the top.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.420
And if you thought the base salaries were
a lot, don’t even get me started on overtime.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:57.520
But, this brings up the question: why in the
world are pilots paid so much?

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.730
BARRIER TO ENTRY:
One of the main reasons that pilots are paid

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.790
so much is because the barrier to entry is
simply so high.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:11.849
Not only do you need to spend years building
up flight hours, but you also need a four

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:14.660
year degree to fly for any of the major airlines.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.531
The airlines have been getting more lenient
with the degree requirement recently, but

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:22.170
they still very much lean in favor of candidates
who have one.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.790
Starting with the degree requirement, this
doesn’t need any explanation because I’m

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.849
sure all of ya’ll are familiar with how
this works.

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:33.170
You go to an institution for 4 or maybe 3
years and reluctantly grind out annoying homeworks

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.240
and tests and eventually, you’ll walk out
with a piece of paper.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.520
But, if you want to become a commercial pilot,
you’re only about half way through.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.319
First, you’ll have to get your private pilot’s
license which costs between $10 to $20,000

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:47.510
and usually takes about 9 months.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.820
After this, you can start grinding out your
commercial pilot’s license which usually

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.430
consists of three parts.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.970
First, you have your instrument ratings which
cost between $10 to $15,000.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:02.030
Second, you have the commercial license itself
which costs $25 to $35,000.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.300
And finally, you have multi engine add-ons
which cost between $5 and $10 grand.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.050
At this point, you’re 2 years in and have
already invested between $50 to $90,000 into

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.590
training, not to mention the cost of college.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.709
But, atleast you have your commercial license
right?

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.530
Well, yes and no.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:23.010
With this license, you can start flying for
commercial purposes, but that doesn’t mean

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.389
that you can actually fly for commercial airlines.

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:30.470
To do that, you have to get your ATPL or airline
transport pilot license.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:35.840
To get this, you don’t need very much additional
training if any, but what you do need is an

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.870
eye watering 1,500 hours.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.960
As a new commercial pilot, you’ll probably
only have somewhere between 250 to 300 hours.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.090
So, you’ll have to find out a way to grind
out another 1,200 hours on your own.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:52.720
Fortunately, if you got a bachelor’s degree
in an aviation related field, you can bring

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.440
down the requirement from 1500 hours to 1000
hours.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.340
This is where good planning comes into play,
but you’d still have to figure out how to

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.310
get another 700 hours.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:07.330
You could opt for a unique solution like crop
dusting or flying skydivers, but more times

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:11.310
than not, the most efficient way to build
up these hours are to become a flight instructor

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.370
yourself.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:17.520
The bad part though is that this will cost
you another $20 to $35,000 and it’ll take

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.090
you atleast another year if not two to actually
build the hours themselves.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:26.410
On the brightside, you will get paid as a
flight instructor, but it won’t be that

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:30.460
much considering how much time and money you’ve
put towards it.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.180
After all this, you’ll finally get your
hands on an airline transport pilot license.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.120
On average, the entire process costs about
a $100,000 and takes four years, so most people

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.710
are usually in their mid 20s by the time they
get their college degree and ATPL.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:49.900
But now, you can finally become a commercial
airline pilot.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.120
PAYING YOUR DUES:
While you can finally get a job as an airline

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.200
pilot, you still wont be making the big bucks.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.550
Given that the aviation industry is 100% driven
by experience, you’re gonna have to start

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.550
off at regional airlines that no one likes.

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.990
The good thing though is that most of these
regional airlines flow directly into the major

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:10.990
airlines.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:15.020
This means that as spots open up at Delta
and American Airlines, they’ll automatically

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.450
transfer the most experienced pilots from
the regionals to them.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:23.960
Usually, this happens once you build somewhere
between 5000 and 6000 hours of flight time.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.380
Given that you built 1000 hours to get your
ATPL, you’ll have to build another 4 to

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.760
5000 hours before you make it to the majors.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.820
To make things worse, this wont be particularly
enjoyable either.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.820
All of the desired routes and schedules are
reserved for the most experienced pilots at

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:39.850
the major airlines.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:45.020
So, as a rookie regional pilot, you’ll be
doing the routes that no one else wants to.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:49.080
This usually consists of multiple 1-2 hour
flights on a daily basis.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.880
Not only will you be taking on the most undesirable
routes, but you’ll also be paid regional

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.300
airlines wages.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.180
Take endeavor airlines for example which flows
through to Delta.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:03.660
As a first year first officer, you’ll only
be making $52 per hour or about $47,000 per

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:04.660
year.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.340
On the brightside, you’ll probably be able
to move up to being a captain within 2 or

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.130
3 years because the churn at these airlines
are super high as everyone is moving up to

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.250
the majors.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:18.750
As a captain at Endeavor, you can earn a much
more respectable $89 per hour or about $80,000

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:19.850
per year.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:23.540
Once you’re a captain, you probably wont
have to wait much longer until the major airlines

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:28.699
call you in, but the entire process at the
regionals usually takes 4 to 5 years in total.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:33.621
So, by the time most people finally get to
the majors, they’re in their late 20s if

00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:35.380
not over 30.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.259
Once you put in a couple of years here though,
you’ll finally become a captain at a major

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:45.009
airline and earn over $200,000 per year which
will slowly rise to $400,000 per year.

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:50.370
So, becoming a pilot is definitely a lucrative
career, but the upfront time and capital investments

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.120
are insane.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.100
Not to mention the daily sacrifices that pilots
have to make like always being on the road

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.800
and constantly eating out.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.520
They’re also taking a massive risk with
their health as well.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.850
Pilots have to pass a first class medical
examination every year, and if they fail,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.190
they can no longer be airline pilots.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:12.330
Fortunately, these guys wont have any trouble
picking up a nice job as an air traffic controller

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.400
or something.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:18.150
But, given that most pilots become pilots
out of passion, this is definitely not an

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.860
optimal outcome.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.199
Airlines have been trying to simplify the
process as much as possible.

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:27.230
For example, they’ve been requesting that
the government reduce the 1500 hour flight

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:33.070
rule, but the FAA just rejected this notion
a few days ago, so that’s not exactly progressing

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.270
well.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:37.849
Airlines have also been launching their own
flight academies that are supposed to streamline

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:42.800
and subsidize the process, but these are brand
new, so we’ll have to wait a few years before

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.050
we see any results.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:48.870
In the meantime, becoming a commercial pilot
remains a long and grueling process.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.910
And to get people to go through this process,
airlines are having to offer late career salaries

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:54.820
well into the multi six figures.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.580
But, given the massive pilot shortage, it
appears that even these salaries are not enough

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:00.770
to attract new talent.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:08.170
PILOT SHORTAGE:
It’s 2022 and we have the most advanced

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:13.530
technology ever, yet for some reason, we also
have shortages all over the place.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:18.459
There’s a car shortage, a chip shortage,
an oil shortage, a minimum wage worker shortage,

00:09:18.459 --> 00:09:22.140
a software engineer shortage, and of course,
a pilot shortage.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.441
Most of these shortages were a result of the
pandemic though.

00:09:24.441 --> 00:09:28.200
The pilot shortage, however, has been ongoing
since 1997.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:34.230
Right now, there’s a shortage of 8,000 pilots
which is about 11% of the workforce.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:41.110
To make things worse, this number is expected
to grow to 30,000 by 2025 and 80,000 by 2032.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:45.420
This insane shortage is giving existing pilots
an extraordinary overtime opportunity.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:50.089
You see, airlines try to avoid cancelling
flights as much as possible because it makes

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.910
them look really bad.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:56.190
People will share their negative experiences
with friends and family and on social media,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.160
and it’s just not a good look.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.820
So, what airlines do to avoid this is ask
existing pilots to take on additional trips

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.980
where they’re paid 100 to 300% more.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:10.661
And when you’re base salary itself is already
over $250 per hour, this quickly jumps up

00:10:10.661 --> 00:10:14.500
to several hundred dollars per hour if not
over a $1000 per hour.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:19.279
Also, pilots are usually extremely picky with
which overtime trips they take because they

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:21.950
can only fly a limited amount of hours per
month.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:25.790
The FAA restricts pilots from flying more
than 100 hours per month.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:30.940
So, after their base 75 hours, they only have
25 hours left to work overtime on a monthly

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:31.940
basis.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.670
Because of this, they usually don’t take
the opportunities that are “only” 100%

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.510
above their base salary.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.650
More times than not, they strategically wait
for the ones that are 200-300% above their

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:42.650
base salary.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:48.150
If they max out their overtime to 25 hours
at $1000 per hour, that’s an extra $25 grand

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:50.990
a month or $300 grand per year.

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:54.959
And this is exactly what many of the top pilots
do.

00:10:54.959 --> 00:11:00.670
Using this strategy, the top pilots at Southwest
are able to make $549,000 per year.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:04.990
The top pilots at Delta are able to make $526,000
per year.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:10.330
And the top pilots at American airlines are
able to cross $700,000 per year.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:13.670
And all of that is only with an 11% pilot
shortage.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:18.110
Imagine how high these salaires will be in
10 years.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:23.029
7 FIGURE INCOMES:
In the end, the reason that pilot’s earn

00:11:23.029 --> 00:11:25.160
a lot isn’t rocket science.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.260
Like any job, they’re paid in accordance
to their demand.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:32.880
Demand for aviation has been skyrocketing
for decades, but the supply of pilots hasn’t

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.080
quite kept up.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.779
The main reason for this is all of the insane
requirements to become a pilot.

00:11:37.779 --> 00:11:42.200
You literally need over a thousand hours worth
of experience before you can get your first

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.490
airline job.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:47.110
This issue has only been accelerated by the
tech industry which has been able to offer

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.519
higher salaries with less education time.

00:11:49.519 --> 00:11:54.790
So, the majority have chosen the tech industry
over the aviation industry, and it’s really

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.380
only the passionate ones that end up as pilots.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:01.870
But, man, if you’re already on the other
side, it’s never been a better time to be

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:02.870
a pilot.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:05.480
The median salary itself currently stands
at $202,000.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:11.740
But, the top pilots earn well above half a
million per year, and honestly it's just a

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.380
matter of time until this becomes 7 figures.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:19.880
Now, personally, I do think all shortages
will eventually be dealt with, but that doesn’t

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.950
mean that the process will be fast.

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:27.720
This shortage can easily take another decade
if not more to sort out, and by then, pilots

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.640
will be one of the most appealing careers
out there in terms of compensation.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:31.690
And that’s why pilots are paid so much.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.060
Would you become a pilot for a 7 figure salary?

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.450
Comment that down below.

